---
title: Symbionts
sidebar:
  label: Symbionts
  order: 7
---

A symbiont is any organism living on or in a host specimen, whether
parasitic, parasitoid, mutualistic, commensal, phoretic, or unknown.

Each symbiont has its own UUID and is linked to its host. The link records how
they interact, where on the host the symbiont was found, and whether the
association is confirmed. Symbionts are available in the `Mammalogy`,
`Ornithology`, `Herpetology`, and `Ichthyology` catalog formats (in
`Invertebrate zoology`, host relationships are recorded in the measurement
form's `Ecological interactions` section).

## Where symbionts live in the specimen form

Symbionts use two places on a specimen record:

- **Examination fields**, at the end of the measurement form, record whether
  the host was checked for symbionts and whether any were found. See
  [Record an examination](#record-an-examination).
- **The symbiont tab**, the bug icon in the `Specimen Parts` card, holds one
  record per symbiont lot or individual. See
  [Add a symbiont record](#add-a-symbiont-record).

Keep both up to date. A host with no symbiont records is ambiguous: it may not
have been examined, or it may have been examined and found clean. The
examination fields remove that ambiguity.

## Before you start

Open `Settings` > `Specimens` and scroll to `Symbionts`. These controlled
vocabularies start with editable defaults and are shared by every project on
this device:

| Setting | Defaults |
| --- | --- |
| `Identification` | `Symbiont ID prefix` and `Symbiont ID number`, used by `New number`. |
| `Interaction types` | `Parasitic`, `Parasitoid`, `Mutualistic`, `Commensal`, `Phoretic`, `Unknown` |
| `Categories` | `Ectosymbiont`, `Endosymbiont`, `Mesosymbiont` |
| `Detection Methods` | `Visual inspection`, `Combing`, `Brushing`, `Ethanol wash`, `Dissection`, `Blood smear`, `Fecal flotation`, `Molecular screening` |
| `Preparation Methods` | `Whole specimen`, `Fluid preserved`, `Slide mounted`, `Pinned`, `Dried`, `Frozen` |
| `Anatomical Locations` | 24 sites from `Head` to `Body cavity`; see [Anatomical locations](#anatomical-locations). |
| `Storage` | `Vial`, `Cryovial`, `Slide`, `Envelope`, `Jar` |
| `Treatments` | The specimen `Treatments` defaults, plus `RNAlater` and `Glycerol` |

Agree on these controlled vocabularies before fieldwork, then share them via
[user config transfer](/en/how-to-recipes/protect-and-collaborate/transfer-user-configs/)
so every device uses the same values. `Setup NAHPU` also includes a `Symbionts`
step for supported catalog formats.

Register the symbiont taxa you expect in the [taxon registry](/en/usages/taxon/)
too. A symbiont's taxon is chosen from the same registry as host taxa, and it
can be a higher rank—such as an order or family—when the symbiont is not yet
identified to species.

## Record an examination

At the end of the measurement form, the `Symbionts` section has three fields:

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Symbionts examined` | `Yes` when the host was checked. Leave it empty when nobody looked. |
| `Symbionts detected` | `Yes` when anything was found, `No` when the host was examined and found clean. |
| `Detection remarks` | How and how thoroughly the host was examined, or why it was not. |

Record `Symbionts examined: Yes` and `Symbionts detected: No` for a clean host.
Do not create an empty symbiont record to say nothing was found.

## Add a symbiont record

{% steps %}

1. Open the host specimen and select the bug icon in the `Specimen Parts` card.
2. Select `Add symbiont`.
3. Choose the `Symbiont taxon`, or leave it as `Not identified`. Use the add
   button beside the field to register a taxon that is not in the list yet.
4. Choose the `Interaction type`. A one-line definition appears below it.
5. Fill in what you know of the rest of the form, then save.
{% /steps %}

A new record starts with the host's preparator as `Collector`, or its
cataloger when no preparator is set, and with the host's collection date as
`Date collected`, or its preparation date when no collection date is set.
Change them when someone else swept, combed, or necropsied the host, or when
the symbiont was taken on another day.

## Symbiont form

The form is grouped into sections. `Show more` reveals
`Preparation & preservation`, `Curation`, and any custom fields hidden by
default.

### IDs

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `UUID` | Assigned by NAHPU and never changes. It becomes the symbiont's `occurrenceID`. |
| `Symbiont ID` | Optional ID for the vial, slide, or envelope. The options menu offers `Scan QR/Barcode` on devices with a camera, `New number`, and `Settings`. |
| `Individuals` | Only for a fish lot with recorded individuals. Links the symbiont to the fish it came from. See [Fishes](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/fishes/#parts-and-symbionts). |

`New number` joins the symbiont ID prefix and the next number, such as `P` and
`101` for `P101`, then increases the number. It is available only while the
field is empty. `Settings` in the same menu edits the prefix and the next
number without leaving the form.

### Symbiont details

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Symbiont taxon` | A registered taxon at any rank, or `Not identified`. |
| `Interaction type` | How the symbiont relates to its host. See [Interaction types and categories](#interaction-types-and-categories). |
| `Category` | Where a parasite or parasitoid lives on its host. Shown only for those interaction types. |
| `Anatomical location` | Where on or in the host the symbiont was found. Narrowed by the category. |
| `Count` | Number of symbiont individuals in this record. |
| `Life stage` | `Egg`, `Larva`, `Nymph`, `Pupa`, `Juvenile`, `Adult`, `Cyst`, `Oocyst`, `Trophozoite`, `Sporozoite`, `Merozoite`, or `Gametocyte`. This list is fixed. |
| `Association status` | `Confirmed` when the symbiont is known to belong to this host, `Suspected` when it may be a contaminant, such as a mite from a shared bag or trap. |

### Collection & identification

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Collector` | Who recovered the symbiont from the host. |
| `Detection method` | How it was found, such as `Combing` or `Blood smear`. |
| `Date collected`, `Time collected` | When it was recovered from the host. |
| `Determiner` | Who identified the symbiont, often a specialist working later. |
| `Date determined` | When it was identified. |
| `Determination method` | How it was identified. Uses the specimen `Identification Methods` controlled vocabulary. |

Collector and determiner are chosen from the project personnel, as on
specimens. Keeping them separate lets a field team record what they collected
while a specialist adds the identification afterward.

### Preparation & preservation

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Preparation method` | How the symbiont was prepared, such as `Slide mounted`. |
| `Treatment` | Preservative or fixative, such as `RNAlater`. |
| `Date preserved`, `Time preserved` | When it was preserved. |

### Curation

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Storage` | Container type, such as `Cryovial`. |
| `Storage location` | Freezer, cabinet, shelf, or box position. |
| `Museum permanent`, `Museum loan` | The museum holding the symbiont, and one receiving it on loan. |
| `Remarks` | Anything else about this symbiont. |

Custom fields placed in the symbiont section of
[custom fields](/en/how-to-recipes/prepare/configure-custom-fields/) appear at
the end of the form.

## Interaction types and categories

`Interaction type` describes the ecological relationship. The defaults are:

| Interaction type | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `Parasitic` | Lives on or in the host and harms it. |
| `Parasitoid` | Develops in the host and eventually kills it. |
| `Mutualistic` | Both symbiont and host benefit. |
| `Commensal` | Symbiont benefits; host is unaffected. |
| `Phoretic` | Uses the host only for transport. |
| `Unknown` | Relationship to the host is not known. |

Noun forms such as `Parasitism`, `Mutualism`, `Commensalism`, and `Phoresy`
are recognized as the same types. Values imported in these forms from other
sources get the matching icon and behavior. You can add your own interaction types in Settings;
they are saved and exported as entered.

`Category` describes where the symbiont lives relative to the host:

- `Ectosymbiont`: on the outside, such as fleas, lice, and ticks.
- `Endosymbiont`: inside, such as helminths in the gut or blood parasites.
- `Mesosymbiont`: in openings and cavities connected to the outside, such as
  the ear, nasal cavity, or gills.

The category is shown only when the interaction type is `Parasitic` or
`Parasitoid`. Changing the interaction type to anything else clears the
category. A record with no interaction type keeps its category, since records
imported or created before interaction types existed may carry only a category.

When you leave `Interaction type` empty, choose `Unknown` instead if the
relationship is genuinely unknown. An empty value means it was not recorded.

## Anatomical locations

Choosing a category narrows `Anatomical location` to sites that fit it:

| Category | Locations offered |
| --- | --- |
| `Ectosymbiont` | Head, Ear, Body, Dorsum, Venter, Limb, Tail, Skin, Fur, Feathers, Gill |
| `Endosymbiont` | Nasal cavity, Oral cavity, Blood, Stomach, Small intestine, Large intestine, Cecum, Liver, Lung, Heart, Kidney, Muscle, Body cavity |
| `Mesosymbiont` | Ear, Nasal cavity, Oral cavity, Gill, Skin |

Without a category, or with a category you added yourself, every location is
offered. Locations you add in `Settings` are never filtered out. Older category
values, such as `Ectoparasite`, `Endoparasite`, and `Mesoparasite`, filter the
options the same way as their symbiont names.

## Symbiont list

The symbiont tab lists one row per record. The icon shows the interaction type,
with a mite icon for records without one or with a custom type. The title shows
the symbiont taxon (or `Unidentified symbiont`), followed by the count and
`Symbiont ID` when set. The subtitle shows linked fish individuals, the
interaction type, the category (for parasitic and parasitoid interactions), the
anatomical location, the life stage, and the preparation method.

Select a row's edit button to change it. To delete records, turn on selection,
check the records, and confirm the delete. Deleting the host specimen deletes
its symbionts too.

## Curation

[Tissue curation](/en/usages/curation/tissue-curation/) can check symbiont
tubes into storage boxes alongside tissues. Turn on `Include symbionts` in its
setup to match symbionts by symbiont ID or by the field ID of their host, and
use `Symbiont category` to limit the match. Box cells label symbionts with a
leading `P` that sets them apart from parts.

## Export and analysis

| Where | What you get |
| --- | --- |
| [Export Records](/en/usages/export/export-records/) | Record type `Symbionts`: one row per symbiont, followed by its host's collecting record. Specimen exports for catalogs with symbionts also add the examination fields and symbiont columns. |
| Data table | The `Symbionts` table shows symbiont ID, taxon, interaction type, category, count, and anatomical location, and can be edited in bulk. |
| [Document templates](/en/usages/export/export-documents/) | Record type `Symbiont`, for vial labels and slide labels. |
| [Statistics](/en/usages/export/export-statistics/) | Measure `Symbionts`, grouped by symbiont taxon, category, anatomical location, or host species. Offered once the project has a symbiont record. |
| [Data packages](/en/usages/export/export-bundles/) | The `symbiont`, `symbiontDetection`, and `symbiontIndividual` tables, plus `vocabularies/symbionts.csv` with your symbiont settings. |

[Project merges](/en/how-to-recipes/protect-and-collaborate/merge-project/)
and imports match symbionts by UUID. A symbiont UUID that is already in the
database is flagged for review instead of being imported twice.

### Darwin Core

In a [Darwin Core archive](/en/how-to-recipes/export-and-print/create-darwin-core-archive/),
each symbiont becomes its own occurrence, linked to its host by an
OrganismInteraction row:

| NAHPU field | Darwin Core |
| --- | --- |
| `UUID` | `occurrenceID` and `organismInteractionID` |
| `Symbiont ID` | `catalogNumber` |
| `Symbiont taxon` | `taxonID`, `scientificName`, and the higher ranks |
| `Collector` | `recordedBy`, `recordedByID` |
| `Determiner`, `Date determined`, `Determination method` | `identifiedBy`, `identifiedByID`, `dateIdentified`, `identificationType` |
| `Count`, `Life stage` | `individualCount`, `lifeStage` |
| `Preparation method`, `Treatment` | `preparations` |
| `Date collected`, `Time collected` | `eventDate`, `eventTime` |
| `Detection method` | `samplingProtocol` |
| `Remarks` | `occurrenceRemarks` |
| `Interaction type` | `organismInteractionType`; a record without one falls back to its category |
| `Category`, `Association status` | `organismInteractionDescription`, when an interaction type is set |
| `Anatomical location` | `relatedOrganismPart` |
| `Individuals` | A `host individual` occurrence assertion |

The symbiont occurrence shares the host's `eventID`, uses `basisOfRecord`
`PreservedSpecimen`, and names its host in `associatedOccurrences`. Storage,
museum, and preservation-date fields have no Darwin Core term and stay in the
NAHPU data package. See the
[Darwin Core audit](/en/contributing/code/database/#symbionts-and-specimen-material)
for every column.

## Records from older versions

NAHPU recorded only parasites before it tracked symbionts. Updating moves those
records into symbionts automatically: every existing record gets the
interaction type `Parasitic`, and its people become a collector and a
determiner. Old category values such as `Ectoparasite` keep working. Exports,
templates, presets, and configs that still use `parasite` names are read with
their symbiont names.
